[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Text "Find expert tools automatically" with a search bar showing "What's trending".
AUDIO: 'The most interesting part of Treg is not the claim that it has thousands of tools. It is the change in buying model. Instead of giving an AI agent a pile of vendor accounts, API keys, and monthly subscriptions, Treg gives the agent one catalog, one token, visible per call'

[00:13] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A slide titled "THE BUYING MODEL" with text "ONE TOKEN. PER CALL." and a screenshot of the Treg interface listing various integrations like Semrush, Majestic, SerpApi.
AUDIO: 'prices, and a way to choose a tool by the job it needs to do. Jason Joe describes it as open router for tools. That comparison is useful, but incomplete. Open router routes model'

[00:26] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Text "Turn 🛋️ into an SDR!" with a search bar showing "Find CMOs of companies that raised funds recently" and a "treg TOOLBELT" with icons for Hunter, Lusha, PDL, Apollo, Crunchbase, Coresignal.
AUDIO: 'requests. Treg is trying to route real work across search, SEO, social, enrichment, ads, scraping, and other APIs. The official launch says the catalog contains roughly 2600 endpoints'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'across about 40 providers. The public landing page currently says 2617 endpoints across 42 providers. Those are first-party counts, not an independent'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inventory audit. The official launch clip makes the pitch clearly. Old software buying was designed for humans. Choose a vendor, accept a subscription, manage another dashboard, and keep'

